            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nIn this text, we begin studying some of the calendars humanity has used, with the help of division and subtraction. Before continuing, we recommend studying the previous texts in this chapter (<htcite0>), as well as the text about our weeks (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b504be6f-2380-4319-add3-c0682c07e886",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Our Week",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nEvery human community has kept track of time, because timekeeping is essential to our lives.\n\nIn this text, we will explore two methods of timekeeping used by different peoples throughout history: one based on the Sun and another based on the Moon (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\nBefore exploring these calendars, here is a question to reflect on:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "736b2d0a-62b5-466a-bedb-74ba12752ae5",
                     "text": "Llyas, M.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ad027885-dbe9-4eb3-9b00-7524d19439c9",
                     "text": "Aslaksen, H.",
                     "page": "pp. 3 - 13",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Are They All Accurate?</b><br /><br />Do all the calendars in use today correctly represent the Earth's orbit around the Sun?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_week",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Week\n\nThe possible origin of the week was already analyzed in a previous text (<htcite0>), so we will not discuss it again here. One fact, however, is worth revisiting, because it will help us in what follows. A week is a period of 7 days; therefore, we use the seven times table to count weeks.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b504be6f-2380-4319-add3-c0682c07e886",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Our Week",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent7\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_gregorian",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Gregorian Calendar\n\nHumanity has created different calendars according to its beliefs and needs. Some of them are available in History Tracers, at the top right, so you can read content using the calendars we are accustomed to in daily life.\n\nIn this section, we discuss the Gregorian calendar.\n\n#### The Shortest Month in the Gregorian Calendar\n\nAccording to the Gregorian calendar, February has 28 days in most years, but 29 days every four years, in what is known as a leap year (<htcite0>).\n\nLooking at the seven times table, we see that 28 is divisible by 7. In other words, that month can contain an exact number of weeks, as shown in **Calculation 1** (<htcite1>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "adf021de-1c26-44d7-a243-b47eac246c61",
                     "text": "Britannica Editors",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Long Division",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"htLongDivisionFormat\"><b>Division format:</b> <button type=\"button\" class=\"htLongDivisionFormatBtn active\" data-htldformat=\"below\">Result below</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"htLongDivisionFormatBtn\" data-htldformat=\"top\">Result on top</button></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"htLongDivision\" style=\"width: 30%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; font-weight: none; border-collapse: collapse;\"><tr><td>&nbsp; <span style=\"color: blue;\">28</span> </td><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black; border-left: 2px solid black;\"> 7 </td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black;\">-  <span style=\"color: blue;\">28</span> </td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 0 </td><td> &nbsp; </td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Division of 28 by 7.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Other Months\n\nAs a consequence of **Calculation 1** and, especially, of the seven times table, any month with between 29 and 31 days cannot contain exactly 4 full weeks.\n\nLet us take a 31-day month as an example:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"htLongDivision\" style=\"width: 30%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; font-weight: none; border-collapse: collapse;\"><tr><td>&nbsp; <span style=\"color: blue;\">31</span> </td><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black; border-left: 2px solid black;\"> 7 </td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black;\">-  <span style=\"color: blue;\">28</span> </td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 3 </td><td> &nbsp; </td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Division of 31 by 7.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Notice that **Calculation 2** confirms there are 3 days beyond the 28 days of 4 full weeks.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgAstro\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgAstro', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Astrolabe showing a calendar with the months in Latin, held in the British Museum. © Trustees of the British Museum (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "9dfc0a42-23c6-4e5d-bd99-bd5f39af7b0a",
                     "text": "The British Museum",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "12",
                        "day": "17"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_arab",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Islamic Calendar\n\nUnlike the Gregorian calendar, the Islamic calendar is a lunar calendar and is not tied to the seasons (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\nAmong the characteristics relevant to this study, the following stand out (<htcite2>):\n- The months have 29 or 30 days;\n- The year has 354 or 355 days;\n- Four consecutive months have 30 days;\n- Three consecutive months have 29 days;\n- A new month begins whenever a new crescent moon is visible.\n\n#### Weeks\n\nArab societies have also used weeks to group days since the first century of their calendar (the 7th century of the Gregorian calendar) (<htcite3>).\n\nWe can therefore also check how many weeks are in the months of the Islamic calendar (**Calculations 3 and 4**).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ad027885-dbe9-4eb3-9b00-7524d19439c9",
                     "text": "Aslaksen, H.",
                     "page": "pp. 10",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "736b2d0a-62b5-466a-bedb-74ba12752ae5",
                     "text": "Llyas, M.",
                     "page": "pp. 425 - 427",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "736b2d0a-62b5-466a-bedb-74ba12752ae5",
                     "text": "Llyas, M.",
                     "page": "pp. 427 - 428",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "96f1335a-b88b-44b0-aa36-052daec52f9b",
                     "text": "Zerubavel, E.",
                     "page": "pp. 25 - 26",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1989",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"htLongDivision\" style=\"width: 30%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; font-weight: none; border-collapse: collapse;\"><tr><td>&nbsp; <span style=\"color: blue;\">29</span> </td><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black; border-left: 2px solid black;\"> 7 </td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black;\">-  <span style=\"color: blue;\">28</span> </td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 1 </td><td> &nbsp; </td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Division of 29 by 7.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"htLongDivision\" style=\"width: 30%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; font-weight: none; border-collapse: collapse;\"><tr><td>&nbsp; <span style=\"color: blue;\">30</span> </td><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black; border-left: 2px solid black;\"> 7 </td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid black;\">-  <span style=\"color: blue;\">28</span> </td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 2 </td><td> &nbsp; </td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 4</b>: Division of 30 by 7.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Because Islamic months have exactly the same length as some Gregorian months, one might expect Islamic months also to have four weeks plus some days (remainders 1 and 2). There is another aspect to explore, however, through a reflection.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: The Century...</b><br /><br />When we look at different calendars around the world — available at the top right of <i>History Tracers</i> — we realize that centuries are not the same length in all of them.<br /><br />Have you ever considered that mentioning only the century, without saying which calendar you mean, gives incomplete information?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_simplify",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Simplifying\n\nBecause we work with different calendars, people need to synchronize dates so that we know exactly what is being referred to.\n\nEven when we are not trying to synchronize calendars with one another, we still need to adjust each calendar carefully, because a solar year is not an exact whole number of days.\n\nLet us look at the solutions that were found.\n\n#### The Leap Year (Gregorian Calendar)\n\nWhen we work with days, as we have done in this and other texts, we are using a relatively broad unit of time, which hides some details. For example, Earth has a year of about 365 days and 6 hours, not exactly 365 days (<htcite0>). To correct this “excess of hours” and keep the determination of the seasons consistent, among other things, the following rules are used to decide whether a year is a leap year (<htcite1>):\n- The year is divisible by 4 but not by 100; or\n- The year is divisible by 400.\n\nRecall that a number is divisible by another when it is a multiple of that number — that is, when the remainder of the division is zero.\n\n#### Conversions\n\nThe differences between the Islamic calendar and the Gregorian calendar led Islamic scholars to create methods so that their shorter calendar could be matched to the solar calendar (<htcite2>). Among these methods, the **Istalahi** stands out, in which one considers: *\"a cycle of 30 lunar years, in which the months alternate between 29 and 30 days, with 11 years having 355 days and the others having 354 days\"* (<htcite3>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "924f3991-5eec-4639-b307-5ce39447f6f6",
                     "text": "NASA",
                     "page": "Terra",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ad027885-dbe9-4eb3-9b00-7524d19439c9",
                     "text": "Aslaksen, H.",
                     "page": "pp. 10",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "736b2d0a-62b5-466a-bedb-74ba12752ae5",
                     "text": "Llyas, M.",
                     "page": "pp. 427 - 428",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "736b2d0a-62b5-466a-bedb-74ba12752ae5",
