      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we learned that humanity uses different calendars, and that these calendars have both points in common and points of difference.\n\nWe also learned that, because of these differences, whenever we mention broader units of time — such as centuries — we must state which calendar we are using, so as not to confuse people who are not accustomed to the calendars we use.\n\nWe also saw that division is an important tool for adjusting our calendars and for knowing, among other things, the right season to plant certain foods. Finally, we observed that in our lives we need to divide by larger numbers, such as 100, to better understand the world around us. These divisions will be addressed in the upcoming texts.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_practicing",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Practicing\n\nAlthough the year — the time it takes for the Earth to return to the same position in its orbit around the Sun (<htcite0>) — is the same for every people, the way humans organize the division of time is different.\n\nTo help with the following exercises, we provide the multiplication tables for 8 and 9, which you will need in them.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "924f3991-5eec-4639-b307-5ce39447f6f6",
                     "text": "NASA",
                     "page": "Terra",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
